a min repro `init.lua`

```lua
local path_package = vim.fn.stdpath("data") .. "/site/"
local mini_path = path_package .. "pack/deps/start/mini.nvim"
if not vim.loop.fs_stat(mini_path) then
  vim.cmd('echo "Installing `mini.nvim`" | redraw')
  local clone_cmd = {
    "git",
    "clone",
    "--filter=blob:none",
    "https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim",
    mini_path,
  }
  vim.fn.system(clone_cmd)
  vim.cmd("packadd mini.nvim | helptags ALL")
  vim.cmd('echo "Installed `mini.nvim`" | redraw')
end

if vim.loader then
  vim.loader.enable()
end

-- Set up 'mini.deps' (customize to your liking)
MiniDeps = require("mini.deps")
MiniDeps.setup({ path = { package = path_package } })
local add, now, later = MiniDeps.add, MiniDeps.now, MiniDeps.later

now(function()
    vim.g.update_blink = true
    add({
      source = "saghen/blink.cmp",
      depends = {
        "echaya/friendly-snippets",
      },
      checkout = "v1.2.0", -- check releases for latest tag
    })
end)

local cmp = require("blink.cmp")
cmp.setup({
  keymap = {
    preset = "none",
    ["<C-e>"] = { "show", "show_documentation", "hide_documentation" },
    ["<Esc>"] = { "cancel", "fallback" },
    ["<CR>"] = { "accept", "fallback" },
    ["<C-u>"] = { "scroll_documentation_up", "fallback" },
    ["<C-d>"] = { "scroll_documentation_down", "fallback" },
    ["<Tab>"] = { "select_next", "snippet_forward", "fallback" },
    ["<S-Tab>"] = { "select_prev", "snippet_backward", "fallback" },
  },
  appearance = {
    use_nvim_cmp_as_default = true,
    nerd_font_variant = "mono",
  },
  fuzzy = {
    prebuilt_binaries = { download = vim.g.update_blink },
    implementation = "rust",
  },
  completion = {
    list = { selection = { preselect = true, auto_insert = true } },
    documentation = {
      auto_show = true,
      auto_show_delay_ms = 250,
    },
  },
  sources = {
    default = { "lsp", "path", "snippets", "buffer" },
    providers = {
      lsp = {
        min_keyword_length = 0, -- Number of characters to trigger porvider
        score_offset = 0, -- Boost/penalize the score of the items
      },
      path = {
        min_keyword_length = 1,
      },
      snippets = {
        min_keyword_length = 2,
        score_offset = 5, -- Boost/penalize the score of the items
      },
      buffer = {
        min_keyword_length = 2,
        max_items = 5,
      },
    },
  },
  cmdline = {
    keymap = {
      preset = "none",
      ["<C-e>"] = { "show", "show_documentation", "hide_documentation" },
      ["<Esc>"] = { "cancel", "fallback" },
      ["<CR>"] = { "accept_and_enter", "fallback" },
      ["<Tab>"] = { "select_next", "fallback" },
      ["<S-Tab>"] = { "select_prev", "fallback" },
    },
    sources = function()
      local type = vim.fn.getcmdtype()
      if type == "/" or type == "?" then
        return { "buffer" }
      end
      if type == ":" or type == "@" then
        return { "cmdline" }
      end
      return {}
    end,
    completion = {
      menu = { auto_show = true },
      list = { selection = { preselect = false, auto_insert = true } },
    },
  },
})

local map_combo = require("mini.keymap").map_combo
local mode = { "i", "c", "x", "s" }
map_combo(mode, "jk", "<BS><BS><Esc>", { delay = 150 })
map_combo("t", "jk", "<BS><BS><C-\\><C-n>", { delay = 150 })
map_combo("n", "jk", "<cmd>wincmd =<cr>k", { delay = 150 })
```

launch nvim => move to the end of the line `local map_combo = require("mini.keymap").map_combo` => jk won't exit the insert mode at very specific locations (end of `map_combo`). Have to say this is a very funny / tricky behavior XD. 

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9ebb5bf4-6756-4ec2-a758-fbde21497095)

> I think you can do it with `vim.fn.search(pattern, flags, stopline=line(.))`. If that's the case maybe you can pass extra `opts` of `gen_step.search_pattern` into `vim.fn.search()`.

@vunhatchuong, I wanted to say that writing custom step that uses `vim.fn.search()` should be enough in this case, but I guess it won't fully work in Insert mode (as it requires cursor adjustment when search forward). I guess it is reasonable to add all of `stopline`, `timeout`, and `skip` to `opts. I'll try to do that tomorrow.

> ... the problem is that `<Tab>` still works as inserting a tab(spaces), the `<S-Tab>` works though.

@jjiangweilan, the setup above that works as expected for me. Make sure that the `<Tab>` key is not overridden some time afterwards. For example, what is the output of `:imap <Tab>`? It should show that it come from 'mini.keymap' module and have "Multi <Tab>' description.

@echasnovski, I think there's an issue with the `ModeChanged` tracking and `vim.cmd('norm! ...')` commands that may be run.

[mini/keymap.lua#L563](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/lua/mini/keymap.lua#L563) is ignoring my `jj` mapping when the `snacks.picker` (haven't tried `mini.picker`, though I will 😉) previewer happens to call `vim.cmd('norm! ...')` commands ([snacks/picker/core/preview.lua#L311](https://github.com/folke/snacks.nvim/blob/main/lua/snacks/picker/core/preview.lua#L311)) in the preview window. The `on_key(watcher)` callback is triggered on the second `j` between the two `ModeChanged` events (first to `n` and then back to `i`).
Can `vim.fn.mode()` be used directly? What's the motivation to use `ModeChanged`? (Perhaps `on_key` would need to be `vim.scheduled` then to wait for the `norm!` to settle before it checks the current mode?)

@echasnovski, I've got a minimal reproduction. It will open a `snacks.picker` on startup. `jj` is mapped to `<Esc>` with a 1000ms delay. Pressing `jj` right away works fine, however once there's ~4 `j`'s in the input field (insert one at a time, waiting a second between), then the `<Esc>` mapping will not be triggered no matter how quick you press `j`. (Until there are no more items, in which case the previewer will not run `ctx.preview:loc()` (which in turn calls the `vim.cmd('norm! ...')` commands).

I tried to get a reproduction without `snacks.picker`, though I was having trouble getting the same timing. 😕 I'm quite confident it's the `norm!` commands being run as a result of the picker (and its previewer) being updated when the `j` is inserted that's causing the `ModeChanged` events to be at exactly the wrong time causing the `j` key to be ignored, despite not really being pressed in normal mode.

```lua
local lazypath = vim.fn.stdpath("data") .. "/lazy/lazy.nvim"
if not vim.uv.fs_stat(lazypath) then
  local lazyrepo = "https://github.com/folke/lazy.nvim.git"
  vim.fn.system({ "git", "clone", "--filter=blob:none", "--branch=stable", lazyrepo, lazypath })
end
vim.opt.rtp:prepend(lazypath)

require("lazy").setup({
  {
    "folke/snacks.nvim",
    config = function()
      vim.schedule(function()
        require("snacks").picker.pick({
          items = {
            { text = "_", pos = { 1, 1 } },
            { text = "_jj", pos = { 1, 1 } },
            { text = "_jjj", pos = { 1, 1 } },
            { text = "_jjjj", pos = { 1, 1 } },
            { text = "_jjjjj", pos = { 1, 1 } },
            { text = "_jjjjjj", pos = { 1, 1 } },
            { text = "_jjjjjjj", pos = { 1, 1 } },
            { text = "_jjjjjjjj", pos = { 1, 1 } },
            { text = "_jjjjjjjjj", pos = { 1, 1 } },
          },
          format = function(item) return { { item.text, "Normal" } } end,
          preview = function(ctx) ctx.preview:loc() end,
        })
      end)
    end,
  },
  {
    "echasnovski/mini.nvim",
    config = function()
      local keymap = require("mini.keymap")
      keymap.map_combo("i", "jj", "<BS><BS><Esc>", { delay = 1000 })
    end,
  },
})
```

> * jk to jump out from terminal mode doesn't work unless I jump out manually using  for once => the issue can be consistently replicated with `snacks_terminal`  for both windows and WSL but not from native terminal launched from cmdline

@echaya, I believe this should be fixed on latest `main`. The symptoms indicate that the issue probably was due to 'snacks.terminal' blocking 'ModeChanged' event when it starter terminal mode. As 'mini.keymap' used it to track current mode (for better performance), it didn't know that it was in terminal. Switching manually once reset that.

Now `new_combo` watchers all use explicit `vim.fn.mode()`. This is makes performance scale worth (O(n) vs O(1) in terms of combos), but hopefully should be good enough still.

> Pressing `jj` right away works fine, however once there's ~4 `j`'s in the input field (insert one at a time, waiting a second between), then the `<Esc>` mapping will not be triggered no matter how quick you press `j`

@garrett-hopper, this should also be fixed on latest `main`. The reason is most certainly was in how combos track their state inside a sequence. Previously doing "Press j - wait beyond delay - type jj quickly" didn't work because combo didn't reset properly at first "j" after long delay. It could only be observed for combos that contain same letter several times, so thanks for using "jj" as "better escape" variant :)

If you still experience issues with 'snacks.picker' and think it might be due to "normal! ...", try on Neovim>=0.11, as it is recommended for `new_combo()` due to how `vim.on_key` can work there.
